# Postgres

The PostgreSQL v3 wire protocol, written entirely in LOGOS on the tcp.lg client
natives — the flagship byte-level protocol library of the language. Every frame
is built and parsed here as a `Seq of Int` of bytes, exactly as the protocol
documents them: length-prefixed messages, big-endian integers, null-terminated
strings. The message tags and authentication codes mirror the PostgreSQL source
(src/include/libpq/protocol.h): Query 'Q', Terminate 'X', PasswordMessage /
SASLInitialResponse / SASLResponse all 'p'; AuthenticationRequest codes 0 (Ok),
3 (CleartextPassword), 5 (MD5Password), 10/11/12 (SASL begin/continue/final).

`pgConnect` speaks the startup handshake with cleartext, MD5
(fe-auth.c's md5(md5(password+user)+salt) double hash), and SCRAM-SHA-256
(RFC 5802/7677 over the SASL frames, exactly libpq's fe-auth-scram.c exchange:
gs2 header "n,,", empty username "n=", channel binding "c=biws", and the
server-nonce-must-extend-ours and server-signature checks). `pgQuery` runs the
simple-query protocol ('Q' → RowDescription/DataRow/CommandComplete/
ReadyForQuery); `pgExecute` returns the rows-affected count from the
CommandComplete tag (PQcmdTuples semantics); `pgClose` sends Terminate and
closes the socket. Errors are values everywhere: an ErrorResponse becomes an
`Err` carrying severity, SQLSTATE code, and message; an unknown tag, a torn
frame, or a refused connect can never hang or trap a program.

The EXTENDED QUERY PROTOCOL is the parameterized path — prepared statements
are how user data reaches SQL, never string interpolation. `pgPrepare` sends
Parse ('P') + Describe ('S') + Sync and captures the declared parameter OIDs
(ParameterDescription 't') and result shape (RowDescription 'T' or NoData
'n'); `pgExecPrepared`/`pgExecPreparedN` send Bind ('B', text-format params,
NULL as length -1 via the nulls mask) + Describe ('P') + Execute ('E',
max_rows 0 = ALL rows, so PortalSuspended never arrives) + Sync, exactly
libpq's PQsendQueryGuts flight (fe-exec.c); `pgQueryParams` is the
unnamed-statement fast path (Parse+Bind+Describe+Execute+Sync in one flight,
PQsendQueryParams). `pgCancelRequest` opens a FRESH connection and sends the
16-byte CancelRequest packet (code 80877102 = PG_PROTOCOL(1234,5678),
pqcomm.h) built from the BackendKeyData pid/secret every connect captures.

NULL is honest here: `QueryResult` carries a `nullMask` parallel to `rows`
(DataRow length -1 → true; a present empty string → false), the `*AtOpt`
getters return `some value`/`none`, and `pgIsNullAt` asks directly. The
LEGACY getters keep their original projection for compatibility: `pgTextAt`
surfaces NULL as `""`, and the typed parsers reject that `""` with a parse
Err — reach for the Opt getters when NULL matters.

## Note
SINGLE-CONNECTION SEMANTICS: a `PgConn` is one socket speaking one strictly-ordered protocol conversation — share it across concurrent tasks and the interleaved frames would corrupt the stream.

Run one statement at a time per connection; open more connections for
parallelism. Connection POOLING is deliberately future work — this version hands
you the honest primitive. THE PARAMETERIZED PATH IS THE PATH: never interpolate
user data into SQL text; put it in `$n` parameters through
pgPrepare/pgExecPrepared/ pgQueryParams. The only SQL this module composes
itself is from a validated closed set (transaction control, `SET
statement_timeout = <int>`).
## Definition

## Note
An open PostgreSQL connection: the tcp.lg handle, the endpoint, and the server's cancel key.

The endpoint is kept for the cancel protocol's fresh connection; the BackendKeyData pid/secret pair is what the startup drains ('K', pqcomm.h
CancelRequestPacket) — 0/0 when the server sent none.

A PgConn has:
    a conn, which is Int.
    a host, which is Text.
    a port, which is Int.
    a backendPid, which is Int.
    a backendSecret, which is Int.

## Note
A parsed connection URL: postgres://user:pass@host:port/database (postgresql:// is an alias).

The port defaults to 5432; the database defaults to the user (libpq's rule); the
password defaults to empty.

A PgUrl has:
    a user, which is Text.
    a pass, which is Text.
    a host, which is Text.
    a port, which is Int.
    a database, which is Text.

## Note
The result of a query: column names and type OIDs, every row as text, the NULL mask, and the command tag.

The result of a query: the column names, their type OIDs (RowDescription
order), every row's values as text (both protocols deliver text format here),
the honest NULL mask parallel to `rows` (DataRow length -1 → true), and the
CommandComplete tag (so DML through the parameterized path reports
rows-affected via pgRowsAffected).

A QueryResult has:
    a columns, which is Seq of Text.
    a typeOids, which is Seq of Int.
    a rows, which is Seq of Seq of Text.
    a nullMask, which is Seq of Seq of Bool.
    a cmdTag, which is Text.

## Note
A prepared statement's captured shape: its parameter OIDs and result columns.

The parameter OIDs are server-declared (ParameterDescription 't') and the result columns come from RowDescription 'T'; `hasRows` is false when Describe
answered NoData 'n' (DML without RETURNING).

A PgPrepared has:
    a stmtName, which is Text.
    a paramOids, which is Seq of Int.
    a columns, which is Seq of Text.
    a typeOids, which is Seq of Int.
    a hasRows, which is Bool.

## Note
A signed Int32 as four big-endian bytes (two's complement, so -1 is ff ff ff ff
— the protocol's NULL column length).

## To pgI32be (n: Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable v be n.
    If v is less than 0:
        Set v to v + 4294967296.
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Push (v / 16777216) % 256 to out.
    Push (v / 65536) % 256 to out.
    Push (v / 256) % 256 to out.
    Push v % 256 to out.
    Return out.

## Note
A signed Int16 as two big-endian bytes (two's complement).

## To pgI16be (n: Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable v be n.
    If v is less than 0:
        Set v to v + 65536.
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Push (v / 256) % 256 to out.
    Push v % 256 to out.
    Return out.

## Note
Reads a signed big-endian Int32 at 1-based offset `off`.

## To pgReadI32be (xs: Seq of Int) and (off: Int) -> Int:
    Let b0 be item off of xs.
    Let b1 be item (off + 1) of xs.
    Let b2 be item (off + 2) of xs.
    Let b3 be item (off + 3) of xs.
    Let mutable v be b0 * 16777216 + b1 * 65536 + b2 * 256 + b3.
    If v is at least 2147483648:
        Set v to v - 4294967296.
    Return v.

## Note
Reads a signed big-endian Int16 at 1-based offset `off`.

## To pgReadI16be (xs: Seq of Int) and (off: Int) -> Int:
    Let b0 be item off of xs.
    Let b1 be item (off + 1) of xs.
    Let mutable v be b0 * 256 + b1.
    If v is at least 32768:
        Set v to v - 65536.
    Return v.

## Note
The protocol's String(s) atom: the UTF-8 bytes of `s` plus the terminating NUL.

## To pgCString (s: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let sb be text_bytes(s).
    Repeat for b in sb:
        Push b to out.
    Push 0 to out.
    Return out.

## Note
Frames a tagged message: the tag byte, the Int32 length (the length counts
itself and the payload, never the tag), then the payload.

## To pgTaggedMessage (tag: Int) and (payload: Seq of Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Push tag to out.
    Let plen be length of payload.
    Let lenBytes be pgI32be(plen + 4).
    Repeat for b in lenBytes:
        Push b to out.
    Repeat for b in payload:
        Push b to out.
    Return out.

## Note
The 8-byte SSLRequest: a length of 8 and the reserved version code 80877103.

PostgreSQL negotiates TLS *before* the startup message and outside the normal framing — the
server answers with a single byte, `S` to proceed or `N` to decline, and only then does the same
socket become a TLS session. That is why `tcpUpgradeTls` exists: a connect-with-TLS call cannot
express it.

## To pgSslRequestMessage () -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let len be pgI32be(8).
    Repeat for b in len:
        Push b to out.
    Let code be pgI32be(80877103).
    Repeat for b in code:
        Push b to out.
    Return out.

## Note
Offers TLS on an open connection and secures it if the server agrees.

`Ok true` means the session is encrypted; `Ok false` means the server declined and the
connection is still usable in the clear. A caller that must not proceed unencrypted checks the
answer — which is why this reports what happened rather than deciding.

## To pgOfferTls (c: Int) and (hostName: Text) -> Result of Bool and Text:
    Let req be pgSslRequestMessage().
    Inspect tcpSend(c, req):
        When Ok (n):
            Let n2 be n.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.
    Inspect tcpRecvExact(c, 1):
        When Ok (reply):
            Let answer be item 1 of reply.
            If answer is equal to 83:
                Inspect tcpUpgradeTls(c, hostName):
                    When Ok (u):
                        Return a new Ok with value true.
                    When Err (e):
                        Return a new Err with error e.
            Return a new Ok with value false.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The StartupMessage — the one UNTAGGED frontend message: Int32 total length,
Int32 protocol version 196608 (3.0), the cstring pairs user/database, and a
trailing terminator byte.

## To pgStartupMessage (userName: Text) and (databaseName: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable payload be a new Seq of Int.
    Let ver be pgI32be(196608).
    Repeat for b in ver:
        Push b to payload.
    Let ku be pgCString("user").
    Repeat for b in ku:
        Push b to payload.
    Let vu be pgCString(userName).
    Repeat for b in vu:
        Push b to payload.
    Let kd be pgCString("database").
    Repeat for b in kd:
        Push b to payload.
    Let vd be pgCString(databaseName).
    Repeat for b in vd:
        Push b to payload.
    Push 0 to payload.
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let plen be length of payload.
    Let lenBytes be pgI32be(plen + 4).
    Repeat for b in lenBytes:
        Push b to out.
    Repeat for b in payload:
        Push b to out.
    Return out.

## Note
The Query message 'Q': the SQL as a cstring.

## To pgQueryMessage (sql: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let payload be pgCString(sql).
    Return pgTaggedMessage(81, payload).

## Note
The PasswordMessage 'p': the (cleartext or md5-hashed) password as a cstring.

## To pgPasswordMessage (password: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let payload be pgCString(password).
    Return pgTaggedMessage(112, payload).

## Note
The Terminate message 'X': an empty body.

## To pgTerminateMessage () -> Seq of Int:
    Let payload be a new Seq of Int.
    Return pgTaggedMessage(88, payload).

## Note
The SASLInitialResponse frame (also tag 'p'): the mechanism name
"SCRAM-SHA-256" as a cstring, the Int32 byte length of the initial client
response, then those bytes verbatim.

## To pgSaslInitialMessage (initial: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable payload be a new Seq of Int.
    Let mech be pgCString("SCRAM-SHA-256").
    Repeat for b in mech:
        Push b to payload.
    Let dataB be text_bytes(initial).
    Let dn be length of dataB.
    Let dlen be pgI32be(dn).
    Repeat for b in dlen:
        Push b to payload.
    Repeat for b in dataB:
        Push b to payload.
    Return pgTaggedMessage(112, payload).

## Note
The SASLResponse frame (also tag 'p'): the client-final message verbatim, no
mechanism and no inner length.

## To pgSaslResponseMessage (final: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let payload be text_bytes(final).
    Return pgTaggedMessage(112, payload).

## Note
Maps a 6-bit value to its RFC 4648 standard-alphabet character byte.

## To pgBase64Char (v: Int) -> Int:
    If v is less than 26:
        Return v + 65.
    If v is less than 52:
        Return v + 71.
    If v is less than 62:
        Return v - 4.
    If v is equal to 62:
        Return 43.
    Return 47.

## Note
Base64 over raw BYTES (RFC 4648, with padding) — SCRAM salts and HMAC proofs
are arbitrary binary, so this works on `Seq of Int`, not on Text.

## To pgBase64 (xs: Seq of Int) -> Text:
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let n be length of xs.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While (i + 2) is at most n:
        Let ba be item i of xs.
        Let bb be item (i + 1) of xs.
        Let bc be item (i + 2) of xs.
        Push pgBase64Char(ba / 4) to out.
        Push pgBase64Char((ba % 4) * 16 + bb / 16) to out.
        Push pgBase64Char((bb % 16) * 4 + bc / 64) to out.
        Push pgBase64Char(bc % 64) to out.
        Set i to i + 3.
    Let rem be n - i + 1.
    If rem is equal to 1:
        Let ra be item i of xs.
        Push pgBase64Char(ra / 4) to out.
        Push pgBase64Char((ra % 4) * 16) to out.
        Push 61 to out.
        Push 61 to out.
    If rem is equal to 2:
        Let sa be item i of xs.
        Let sb be item (i + 1) of xs.
        Push pgBase64Char(sa / 4) to out.
        Push pgBase64Char((sa % 4) * 16 + sb / 16) to out.
        Push pgBase64Char((sb % 16) * 4) to out.
        Push 61 to out.
    Return text_from_bytes(out).

## Note
Maps an RFC 4648 character byte back to its 6-bit value, or -1 for padding and
anything else (decode simply stops consuming such bytes).

## To pgBase64Val (ch: Int) -> Int:
    If ch is at least 65:
        If ch is at most 90:
            Return ch - 65.
    If ch is at least 97:
        If ch is at most 122:
            Return ch - 71.
    If ch is at least 48:
        If ch is at most 57:
            Return ch + 4.
    If ch is equal to 43:
        Return 62.
    If ch is equal to 47:
        Return 63.
    Return -1.

## Note
Decodes RFC 4648 base64 into raw bytes, ignoring padding.

## To pgBase64Decode (s: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let sb be text_bytes(s).
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let mutable acc be 0.
    Let mutable bits be 0.
    Repeat for ch in sb:
        Let v be pgBase64Val(ch).
        If v is at least 0:
            Set acc to acc * 64 + v.
            Set bits to bits + 6.
            If bits is at least 8:
                Set bits to bits - 8.
                Let mutable dv be 1.
                If bits is equal to 2:
                    Set dv to 4.
                If bits is equal to 4:
                    Set dv to 16.
                Push (acc / dv) % 256 to out.
                Set acc to acc % dv.
    Return out.

## Note
The libpq MD5 double hash (fe-auth.c pg_md5_encrypt): "md5" followed by the
lowercase hex of md5(hex(md5(password+user)) + salt).

## To pgMd5Password (password: Text) and (userName: Text) and (salt: Seq of Int) -> Text:
    Let mutable stage1 be a new Seq of Int.
    Let pb be text_bytes(password).
    Repeat for b in pb:
        Push b to stage1.
    Let ub be text_bytes(userName).
    Repeat for b in ub:
        Push b to stage1.
    Let innerHex be bytesToHex(md5(stage1)).
    Let mutable stage2 be a new Seq of Int.
    Let ib be text_bytes(innerHex).
    Repeat for b in ib:
        Push b to stage2.
    Repeat for b in salt:
        Push b to stage2.
    Let outerHex be bytesToHex(md5(stage2)).
    Return "md5{outerHex}".

## Note
The SCRAM-SHA-256 ClientProof of RFC 5802.

SaltedPassword = Hi(password, salt, iters)
via pbkdf2Sha256; ClientKey = HMAC(SaltedPassword, "Client Key"); StoredKey =
SHA-256(ClientKey); ClientSignature = HMAC(StoredKey, AuthMessage); the proof is
ClientKey XOR ClientSignature.
## To scramClientProof (password: Seq of Int) and (salt: Seq of Int) and (iters: Int) and (authMessage: Seq of Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let sp be pbkdf2Sha256(password, salt, iters).
    Let ckLabel be text_bytes("Client Key").
    Let ck be hmacSha256(sp, ckLabel).
    Let stk be sha256(ck).
    Let cs be hmacSha256(stk, authMessage).
    Let proof be a new Seq of Int.
    Repeat for i from 1 to 32:
        Push ((item i of ck) xor (item i of cs)) to proof.
    Return proof.

## Note
The SCRAM-SHA-256 ServerSignature of RFC 5802: HMAC(ServerKey, AuthMessage).

ServerKey = HMAC(SaltedPassword, "Server Key"). The server's final v=
value must equal it, proving the server also knows the password.
## To scramServerSignature (password: Seq of Int) and (salt: Seq of Int) and (iters: Int) and (authMessage: Seq of Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let sp be pbkdf2Sha256(password, salt, iters).
    Let skLabel be text_bytes("Server Key").
    Let sk be hmacSha256(sp, skLabel).
    Return hmacSha256(sk, authMessage).

## Note
A fresh client nonce: 18 random bytes (libpq's SCRAM_RAW_NONCE_LEN),
base64-encoded so every byte is printable and comma-free as SCRAM requires.

## To pgScramClientNonce () -> Text:
    Let raw be a new Seq of Int.
    Repeat for i from 1 to 18:
        Push randomInt(0, 255) to raw.
    Return pgBase64(raw).

## Note
The rows-affected count of a CommandComplete tag (PQcmdTuples semantics): the
last space-separated word when it is numeric ("INSERT 0 3" is 3, "UPDATE 7" is
7), otherwise 0 ("CREATE TABLE", "BEGIN").

## To pgRowsAffected (tag: Text) -> Int:
    Let parts be split(tag, " ").
    Let np be length of parts.
    If np is less than 2:
        Return 0.
    Let lastPart be item np of parts.
    If allDigits(lastPart) is equal to false:
        Return 0.
    Return parseInt(lastPart).

## Note
Parses postgres://user:pass@host:port/database (postgresql:// alias accepted).

The port defaults to 5432, the database to the user, the password to "". The
user is required; a malformed port is an Err, never a trap.
## To pgParseUrl (url: Text) -> Result of PgUrl and Text:
    Let mutable rest be "".
    Let mutable schemeOk be false.
    If startsWith(url, "postgres://") is equal to true:
        Set rest to removePrefix(url, "postgres://").
        Set schemeOk to true.
    If startsWith(url, "postgresql://") is equal to true:
        Set rest to removePrefix(url, "postgresql://").
        Set schemeOk to true.
    If schemeOk is equal to false:
        Return a new Err with error "pgParseUrl: the url must start with postgres:// or postgresql://".
    Let atIdx be lastIndexOf(rest, "@").
    If atIdx is equal to 0:
        Return a new Err with error "pgParseUrl: the url must include a user (postgres://user@host)".
    Let rb be text_bytes(rest).
    Let rn be length of rb.
    Let mutable credsB be a new Seq of Int.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is less than atIdx:
        Push item i of rb to credsB.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Let mutable hostB be a new Seq of Int.
    Set i to atIdx + 1.
    While i is at most rn:
        Push item i of rb to hostB.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Let creds be text_from_bytes(credsB).
    Let mutable hostPart be text_from_bytes(hostB).
    Let mutable userVal be creds.
    Let mutable passVal be "".
    Let colonIdx be indexOf(creds, ":").
    If colonIdx is greater than 0:
        Set userVal to cutBefore(creds, ":").
        Set passVal to cutAfter(creds, ":").
    If userVal is equal to "":
        Return a new Err with error "pgParseUrl: the url must include a user (postgres://user@host)".
    Let qIdx be indexOf(hostPart, "?").
    If qIdx is greater than 0:
        Set hostPart to cutBefore(hostPart, "?").
    Let mutable hostPort be hostPart.
    Let mutable dbVal be "".
    Let slashIdx be indexOf(hostPart, "/").
    If slashIdx is greater than 0:
        Set hostPort to cutBefore(hostPart, "/").
        Set dbVal to cutAfter(hostPart, "/").
    Let mutable hostVal be hostPort.
    Let mutable portVal be 5432.
    Let portColon be indexOf(hostPort, ":").
    If portColon is greater than 0:
        Set hostVal to cutBefore(hostPort, ":").
        Let portText be cutAfter(hostPort, ":").
        If allDigits(portText) is equal to false:
            Return a new Err with error "pgParseUrl: the port must be a number".
        Set portVal to parseInt(portText).
    If hostVal is equal to "":
        Return a new Err with error "pgParseUrl: the url must include a host".
    If dbVal is equal to "":
        Set dbVal to userVal.
    Let parsed be a new PgUrl with user userVal and pass passVal and host hostVal and port portVal and database dbVal.
    Return a new Ok with value parsed.

## Note
The bytes of a received frame after the tag: items 2..end.

## To pgFrameBody (frame: Seq of Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let n be length of frame.
    Let mutable i be 2.
    While i is at most n:
        Push item i of frame to out.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return out.

## Note
The bytes of `xs` from 1-based offset `off` to the end.

## To pgSliceFrom (xs: Seq of Int) and (off: Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let n be length of xs.
    Let mutable i be off.
    While i is at most n:
        Push item i of xs to out.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return out.

## Note
Reads a cstring at 1-based `off`: the text before the NUL and the offset just
past it.

## To pgReadCStringAt (xs: Seq of Int) and (off: Int) -> (Text, Int):
    Let out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let n be length of xs.
    Let mutable i be off.
    While i is at most n:
        Let b be item i of xs.
        If b is equal to 0:
            Let sDone be text_from_bytes(out).
            Return (sDone, i + 1).
        Push b to out.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Let sAll be text_from_bytes(out).
    Return (sAll, i).

## Note
Renders an ErrorResponse/NoticeResponse body as "SEVERITY SQLSTATE: message"
from its (field-type byte, cstring) pairs — S severity, C code, M message.

## To pgErrorText (payload: Seq of Int) -> Text:
    Let mutable sev be "".
    Let mutable sqlstate be "".
    Let mutable msg be "".
    Let n be length of payload.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most n:
        Let ft be item i of payload.
        If ft is equal to 0:
            Set i to n + 1.
        If ft is greater than 0:
            Let (fv, nxt) be pgReadCStringAt(payload, i + 1).
            If ft is equal to 83:
                Set sev to fv.
            If ft is equal to 67:
                Set sqlstate to fv.
            If ft is equal to 77:
                Set msg to fv.
            Set i to nxt.
    Return "{sev} {sqlstate}: {msg}".

## Note
Receives one backend message: the tag byte followed by the length-counted body.

The returned Seq is the tag at item 1 and the body from item 2 on — a torn frame
or a malformed length surfaces as an Err, never a hang or a truncation.
## To pgRecvMessage (c: Int) -> Result of Seq of Int and Text:
    Let tagR be tcpRecvExact(c, 1).
    Inspect tagR:
        When Ok (tagBytes):
            Let lenR be tcpRecvExact(c, 4).
            Inspect lenR:
                When Ok (lenBytes):
                    Let bodyLen be pgReadI32be(lenBytes, 1) - 4.
                    If bodyLen is less than 0:
                        Return a new Err with error "pg: the server sent a malformed message length".
                    Let mutable frame be a new Seq of Int.
                    Push item 1 of tagBytes to frame.
                    If bodyLen is greater than 0:
                        Let bodyR be tcpRecvExact(c, bodyLen).
                        Inspect bodyR:
                            When Ok (body):
                                Repeat for b in body:
                                    Push b to frame.
                            When Err (e):
                                Return a new Err with error e.
                    Return a new Ok with value frame.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The SCRAM-SHA-256 SASL exchange (RFC 5802) with mutual authentication — a wrong server signature is rejected.

The SCRAM-SHA-256 SASL exchange (libpq fe-auth-scram.c): verify the server
offers SCRAM-SHA-256, send the client-first message with a fresh nonce, check
the server nonce extends ours, prove the password, and verify the server's
signature — a wrong ServerSignature is rejected (mutual authentication).

## To pgScramAuth (c: Int) and (password: Text) and (mechPayload: Seq of Int) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Let mn be length of mechPayload.
    Let mutable seen be false.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    Let mutable scanning be true.
    While scanning is equal to true:
        If i is greater than mn:
            Set scanning to false.
        If scanning is equal to true:
            Let mb be item i of mechPayload.
            If mb is equal to 0:
                Set scanning to false.
            If mb is greater than 0:
                Let (mech, nxt) be pgReadCStringAt(mechPayload, i).
                If mech is equal to "SCRAM-SHA-256":
                    Set seen to true.
                Set i to nxt.
    If seen is equal to false:
        Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: the server offered no SCRAM-SHA-256 mechanism".
    Let clientNonce be pgScramClientNonce().
    Let clientFirstBare be "n=,r={clientNonce}".
    Let clientFirst be "n,,{clientFirstBare}".
    Let initialFrame be pgSaslInitialMessage(clientFirst).
    Let sent1 be tcpSend(c, initialFrame).
    Inspect sent1:
        When Ok (n1):
            Let ok1 be 0.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: {e}".
    Let m1 be pgRecvMessage(c).
    Let mutable serverFirst be "".
    Inspect m1:
        When Ok (frame):
            Let t1 be item 1 of frame.
            If t1 is equal to 69:
                Let body be pgFrameBody(frame).
                Let etext be pgErrorText(body).
                Return a new Err with error etext.
            If t1 is equal to 82:
                Let body be pgFrameBody(frame).
                Let code be pgReadI32be(body, 1).
                If code is equal to 11:
                    Let dataPart be pgSliceFrom(body, 5).
                    Set serverFirst to text_from_bytes(dataPart).
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: {e}".
    If serverFirst is equal to "":
        Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: expected the SCRAM server-first message".
    Let mutable combined be "".
    Let mutable saltB64 be "".
    Let mutable itersText be "".
    Let sfParts be split(serverFirst, ",").
    Repeat for part in sfParts:
        If startsWith(part, "r=") is equal to true:
            Set combined to removePrefix(part, "r=").
        If startsWith(part, "s=") is equal to true:
            Set saltB64 to removePrefix(part, "s=").
        If startsWith(part, "i=") is equal to true:
            Set itersText to removePrefix(part, "i=").
    If startsWith(combined, clientNonce) is equal to false:
        Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: the server nonce does not extend the client nonce".
    If allDigits(itersText) is equal to false:
        Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: the server sent a malformed iteration count".
    Let iters be parseInt(itersText).
    If iters is less than 1:
        Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: the server sent a malformed iteration count".
    Let scramSalt be pgBase64Decode(saltB64).
    Let sn be length of scramSalt.
    If sn is equal to 0:
        Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: the server sent an empty salt".
    Let clientFinalNoProof be "c=biws,r={combined}".
    Let authMessage be "{clientFirstBare},{serverFirst},{clientFinalNoProof}".
    Let authBytes be text_bytes(authMessage).
    Let passBytes be text_bytes(password).
    Let proof be scramClientProof(passBytes, scramSalt, iters, authBytes).
    Let proofB64 be pgBase64(proof).
    Let clientFinal be "{clientFinalNoProof},p={proofB64}".
    Let finalFrame be pgSaslResponseMessage(clientFinal).
    Let sent2 be tcpSend(c, finalFrame).
    Inspect sent2:
        When Ok (n2):
            Let ok2 be 0.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: {e}".
    Let m2 be pgRecvMessage(c).
    Let mutable serverFinal be "".
    Inspect m2:
        When Ok (frame):
            Let t2 be item 1 of frame.
            If t2 is equal to 69:
                Let body be pgFrameBody(frame).
                Let etext be pgErrorText(body).
                Return a new Err with error etext.
            If t2 is equal to 82:
                Let body be pgFrameBody(frame).
                Let code be pgReadI32be(body, 1).
                If code is equal to 12:
                    Let dataPart be pgSliceFrom(body, 5).
                    Set serverFinal to text_from_bytes(dataPart).
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: {e}".
    If startsWith(serverFinal, "v=") is equal to false:
        Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: expected the SCRAM server-final message".
    Let gotSig be removePrefix(serverFinal, "v=").
    Let expectedSigBytes be scramServerSignature(passBytes, scramSalt, iters, authBytes).
    Let expectedSig be pgBase64(expectedSigBytes).
    If gotSig is equal to expectedSig:
        Return a new Ok with value 0.
    Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: the server signature does not verify".

## Note
The post-startup authentication loop: answers every AuthenticationRequest and finishes at ReadyForQuery.

The post-startup authentication loop: answer AuthenticationRequest frames
(Ok/cleartext/MD5/SASL), drain ParameterStatus, CAPTURE BackendKeyData's
pid/secret (the cancel protocol's key, protocol.h 'K'), surface an
ErrorResponse as an Err, and finish at ReadyForQuery.

## To pgAuthLoop (c: Int) and (userName: Text) and (password: Text) and (hostVal: Text) and (portVal: Int) -> Result of PgConn and Text:
    Let mutable bkPid be 0.
    Let mutable bkSecret be 0.
    Let mutable looping be true.
    While looping is equal to true:
        Let mR be pgRecvMessage(c).
        Inspect mR:
            When Ok (frame):
                Let tg be item 1 of frame.
                Let body be pgFrameBody(frame).
                Let mutable handled be false.
                If tg is equal to 82:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Let code be pgReadI32be(body, 1).
                    Let mutable authKnown be false.
                    If code is equal to 0:
                        Set authKnown to true.
                    If code is equal to 3:
                        Set authKnown to true.
                        Let pwFrame be pgPasswordMessage(password).
                        Let sentPw be tcpSend(c, pwFrame).
                        Inspect sentPw:
                            When Ok (ns):
                                Let okc be 0.
                            When Err (e):
                                Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: {e}".
                    If code is equal to 5:
                        Set authKnown to true.
                        Let md5Salt be pgSliceFrom(body, 5).
                        Let hashed be pgMd5Password(password, userName, md5Salt).
                        Let md5Frame be pgPasswordMessage(hashed).
                        Let sentMd5 be tcpSend(c, md5Frame).
                        Inspect sentMd5:
                            When Ok (nm):
                                Let okm be 0.
                            When Err (e):
                                Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: {e}".
                    If code is equal to 10:
                        Set authKnown to true.
                        Let mechList be pgSliceFrom(body, 5).
                        Let sasl be pgScramAuth(c, password, mechList).
                        Inspect sasl:
                            When Ok (nz):
                                Let oks be 0.
                            When Err (e):
                                Return a new Err with error e.
                    If authKnown is equal to false:
                        Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: unsupported authentication request {code}".
                If tg is equal to 83:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 75:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Let keyLen be length of body.
                    If keyLen is at least 8:
                        Set bkPid to pgReadI32be(body, 1).
                        Set bkSecret to pgReadI32be(body, 5).
                If tg is equal to 78:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 69:
                    Let etext be pgErrorText(body).
                    Return a new Err with error etext.
                If tg is equal to 90:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set looping to false.
                If handled is equal to false:
                    Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: unexpected message tag {tg}".
            When Err (e):
                Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: {e}".
    Let cleared be tcpSetTimeout(c, 0).
    Let made be a new PgConn with conn c and host hostVal and port portVal and backendPid bkPid and backendSecret bkSecret.
    Return a new Ok with value made.

## Note
The connect/auth deadline in milliseconds: the whole startup handshake must finish within it.

The handshake runs startup → authentication → ReadyForQuery; past the deadline every blocking recv on the socket surfaces `tcp:
operation timed out` as an Err. It is BOUNDED WELL BELOW PostgreSQL's server-
side `authentication_timeout` (postmaster.c `AuthenticationTimeout`, default
60000 ms) so a failed SCRAM (or any silent / half-closed peer mid-handshake)
refuses PROMPTLY on the client instead of blocking on a dead socket until the
server's timeout fires. Cleared to 0 (no deadline) the moment ReadyForQuery
arrives — a live query blocks as long as it needs.
## To pgConnectDeadlineMillis () -> Int:
    Return 5000.

## Note
Opens a connection from a postgres:// URL and authenticates; every failure (or a stalled handshake) is a bounded `Err`.

Opens a connection from a postgres:// URL: parse, tcp-connect, arm the
connect/auth deadline, send the StartupMessage, authenticate (cleartext, MD5,
or SCRAM-SHA-256 as the server demands), and drain to ReadyForQuery — which
clears the deadline. Every failure is an Err value, and a stalled handshake is
a BOUNDED Err, never a hang on a closed socket.

## Note
Connects in the clear. See `pgConnectSecure` for a connection that requires TLS — which is what
every managed PostgreSQL (RDS, Neon, Supabase, Cloud SQL) needs.

★ THIS ONE DOES NOT NEGOTIATE, DELIBERATELY. Offering TLS here would change the bytes every
existing caller puts on the wire before its first message, and a server or a proxy that does not
expect an SSLRequest sees a protocol error rather than a connection. Security that arrives by
silently altering an existing handshake is not a gift; it is a breaking change wearing one.

## To pgConnect (url: Text) -> Result of PgConn and Text:
    Let parsed be pgParseUrl(url).
    Inspect parsed:
        When Ok (u):
            Let hostVal be u's host.
            Let portVal be u's port.
            Let cR be tcpConnect(hostVal, portVal).
            Inspect cR:
                When Ok (c):
                    Let armed be tcpSetTimeout(c, pgConnectDeadlineMillis()).
                    Inspect armed:
                        When Ok (au):
                            Let ok0 be 0.
                        When Err (e):
                            Let closedAnyway be tcpClose(c).
                            Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: {e}".
                    Let userName be u's user.
                    Let dbName be u's database.
                    Let hello be pgStartupMessage(userName, dbName).
                    Let sent be tcpSend(c, hello).
                    Inspect sent:
                        When Ok (n):
                            Let passVal be u's pass.
                            Return pgAuthLoop(c, userName, passVal, hostVal, portVal).
                        When Err (e):
                            Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: {e}".
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error "pgConnect: {e}".
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
Connects and REQUIRES TLS: the SSLRequest is offered before the startup message, and a server
that declines is an `Err` rather than a quiet fall back to cleartext.

★ REQUIRE, NOT PREFER. A "prefer" mode that continues in the clear when the server says `N` can
be defeated by anything in the path that can say `N` — which is exactly the position an attacker
occupies. A caller that genuinely wants either uses `pgConnect` and `pgOfferTls` and decides for
itself; this one answers a connection that is encrypted or no connection at all.

## To pgConnectSecure (url: Text) -> Result of PgConn and Text:
    Inspect pgParseUrl(url):
        When Ok (u):
            Let hostVal be u's host.
            Let portVal be u's port.
            Inspect tcpConnect(hostVal, portVal):
                When Ok (c):
                    Inspect tcpSetTimeout(c, pgConnectDeadlineMillis()):
                        When Ok (au):
                            Let armedOk be 0.
                        When Err (e):
                            Let closedArm be tcpClose(c).
                            Return a new Err with error "pgConnectSecure: {e}".
                    Inspect pgOfferTls(c, hostVal):
                        When Ok (secured):
                            If secured is equal to false:
                                Let closedPlain be tcpClose(c).
                                Return a new Err with error "pgConnectSecure: the server declined TLS".
                        When Err (e):
                            Let closedTls be tcpClose(c).
                            Return a new Err with error "pgConnectSecure: {e}".
                    Let userName be u's user.
                    Let dbName be u's database.
                    Inspect tcpSend(c, pgStartupMessage(userName, dbName)):
                        When Ok (n):
                            Return pgAuthLoop(c, userName, u's pass, hostVal, portVal).
                        When Err (e):
                            Let closedSend be tcpClose(c).
                            Return a new Err with error "pgConnectSecure: {e}".
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error "pgConnectSecure: {e}".
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
Runs one simple query 'Q' and collects its result.

Column names and type OIDs come from RowDescription and rows (text values)
from DataRow. An ErrorResponse becomes the Err after the protocol drains to
ReadyForQuery (the connection stays usable); NoticeResponse and ParameterStatus
are skipped; an unknown tag is a clean Err.
## To pgQuery (pgc: PgConn) and (sql: Text) -> Result of QueryResult and Text:
    Let c be pgc's conn.
    Let q be pgQueryMessage(sql).
    Let sentQ be tcpSend(c, q).
    Inspect sentQ:
        When Ok (n0):
            Let okq be 0.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "pgQuery: {e}".
    Let mutable cols be a new Seq of Text.
    Let mutable oids be a new Seq of Int.
    Let mutable rws be a new Seq of Seq of Text.
    Let mutable masks be a new Seq of Seq of Bool.
    Let mutable tagVal be "".
    Let mutable errText be "".
    Let mutable looping be true.
    While looping is equal to true:
        Let mR be pgRecvMessage(c).
        Inspect mR:
            When Ok (frame):
                Let tg be item 1 of frame.
                Let body be pgFrameBody(frame).
                Let mutable handled be false.
                If tg is equal to 84:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Let (rdNames, rdOids) be pgParseRowDescription(body).
                    Set cols to rdNames.
                    Set oids to rdOids.
                If tg is equal to 68:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Let (rowCells, rowMask) be pgParseDataRow(body).
                    Push rowCells to rws.
                    Push rowMask to masks.
                If tg is equal to 67:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Let (tagText, tEnd) be pgReadCStringAt(body, 1).
                    Set tagVal to tagText.
                If tg is equal to 73:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 78:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 83:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 69:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set errText to pgErrorText(body).
                If tg is equal to 90:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set looping to false.
                If handled is equal to false:
                    Return a new Err with error "pgQuery: unexpected message tag {tg}".
            When Err (e):
                Return a new Err with error "pgQuery: {e}".
    If errText is equal to "":
        Let qr be a new QueryResult with columns cols and typeOids oids and rows rws and nullMask masks and cmdTag tagVal.
        Return a new Ok with value qr.
    Return a new Err with error errText.

## Note
Runs a statement and returns the rows-affected count parsed from the CommandComplete tag.

Same drain discipline as pgQuery: an ErrorResponse is collected, the protocol
still drains to ReadyForQuery, then the Err surfaces.
## To pgExecute (pgc: PgConn) and (sql: Text) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Let c be pgc's conn.
    Let q be pgQueryMessage(sql).
    Let sentQ be tcpSend(c, q).
    Inspect sentQ:
        When Ok (n0):
            Let okq be 0.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "pgExecute: {e}".
    Let mutable cmdTag be "".
    Let mutable errText be "".
    Let mutable looping be true.
    While looping is equal to true:
        Let mR be pgRecvMessage(c).
        Inspect mR:
            When Ok (frame):
                Let tg be item 1 of frame.
                Let body be pgFrameBody(frame).
                Let mutable handled be false.
                If tg is equal to 67:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Let (tagText, tEnd) be pgReadCStringAt(body, 1).
                    Set cmdTag to tagText.
                If tg is equal to 84:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 68:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 73:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 78:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 83:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 69:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set errText to pgErrorText(body).
                If tg is equal to 90:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set looping to false.
                If handled is equal to false:
                    Return a new Err with error "pgExecute: unexpected message tag {tg}".
            When Err (e):
                Return a new Err with error "pgExecute: {e}".
    If errText is equal to "":
        Let affected be pgRowsAffected(cmdTag).
        Return a new Ok with value affected.
    Return a new Err with error errText.

## Note
Sends Terminate 'X' and closes the socket. The handle is dead afterwards.

## To pgClose (pgc: PgConn) -> Result of Unit and Text:
    Let c be pgc's conn.
    Let bye be pgTerminateMessage().
    Let sentBye be tcpSend(c, bye).
    Inspect sentBye:
        When Ok (nb):
            Let okb be 0.
        When Err (e):
            Let oke be 0.
    Return tcpClose(c).

## Note
The raw text of a cell, 1-based row and column, bounds-checked.

## To pgCellAt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Text and Text:
    Let rws be qr's rows.
    Let nr be length of rws.
    If rowIdx is less than 1:
        Return a new Err with error "pg: row {rowIdx} is out of range (1..{nr})".
    If rowIdx is greater than nr:
        Return a new Err with error "pg: row {rowIdx} is out of range (1..{nr})".
    Let row be item rowIdx of rws.
    Let nc be length of row.
    If colIdx is less than 1:
        Return a new Err with error "pg: column {colIdx} is out of range (1..{nc})".
    If colIdx is greater than nc:
        Return a new Err with error "pg: column {colIdx} is out of range (1..{nc})".
    Let cell be item colIdx of row.
    Return a new Ok with value cell.

## Note
The cell as Text (PostgreSQL's simple-query values are already text).

## To pgTextAt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Text and Text:
    Return pgCellAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).

## Note
True when `s` is a decimal integer: an optional leading minus, then digits.

## To pgIsIntText (s: Text) -> Bool:
    Let sb be text_bytes(s).
    Let n be length of sb.
    If n is equal to 0:
        Return false.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    If (item 1 of sb) is equal to 45:
        If n is equal to 1:
            Return false.
        Set i to 2.
    While i is at most n:
        Let b be item i of sb.
        If b is less than 48:
            Return false.
        If b is greater than 57:
            Return false.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return true.

## Note
True when `s` is a decimal real: optional sign, digits, optional fraction,
optional e/E exponent with optional sign — the shapes float8's text output takes.

## To pgIsRealText (s: Text) -> Bool:
    Let sb be text_bytes(s).
    Let n be length of sb.
    If n is equal to 0:
        Return false.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    Let mutable sawDigit be false.
    Let mutable sawDot be false.
    Let mutable sawExp be false.
    If (item 1 of sb) is equal to 45:
        Set i to 2.
    While i is at most n:
        Let b be item i of sb.
        Let mutable okb be false.
        If b is at least 48:
            If b is at most 57:
                Set okb to true.
                Set sawDigit to true.
        If b is equal to 46:
            If sawDot is equal to false:
                If sawExp is equal to false:
                    Set okb to true.
                    Set sawDot to true.
        If b is equal to 101:
            If sawExp is equal to false:
                If sawDigit is equal to true:
                    Set okb to true.
                    Set sawExp to true.
                    Set sawDigit to false.
                    If (i + 1) is at most n:
                        Let nb be item (i + 1) of sb.
                        If nb is equal to 43:
                            Set i to i + 1.
                        If nb is equal to 45:
                            Set i to i + 1.
        If b is equal to 69:
            If sawExp is equal to false:
                If sawDigit is equal to true:
                    Set okb to true.
                    Set sawExp to true.
                    Set sawDigit to false.
                    If (i + 1) is at most n:
                        Let nb be item (i + 1) of sb.
                        If nb is equal to 43:
                            Set i to i + 1.
                        If nb is equal to 45:
                            Set i to i + 1.
        If okb is equal to false:
            Return false.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return sawDigit.

## Note
The cell parsed as an Int; a non-integer cell is an Err, never a trap.

## To pgIntAt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Let cellR be pgCellAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect cellR:
        When Ok (cell):
            If pgIsIntText(cell) is equal to false:
                Return a new Err with error "pg: the value '{cell}' is not an Int".
            Let parsedInt be parseInt(cell).
            Return a new Ok with value parsedInt.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The cell parsed as a Bool: PostgreSQL's text bools "t"/"f" (and "true"/"false").

## To pgBoolAt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Bool and Text:
    Let cellR be pgCellAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect cellR:
        When Ok (cell):
            If cell is equal to "t":
                Return a new Ok with value true.
            If cell is equal to "true":
                Return a new Ok with value true.
            If cell is equal to "f":
                Return a new Ok with value false.
            If cell is equal to "false":
                Return a new Ok with value false.
            Return a new Err with error "pg: the value '{cell}' is not a Bool".
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The cell parsed as a Real; a non-numeric cell is an Err, never a trap.

## To pgRealAt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Real and Text:
    Let cellR be pgCellAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect cellR:
        When Ok (cell):
            If pgIsRealText(cell) is equal to false:
                Return a new Err with error "pg: the value '{cell}' is not a Real".
            Let parsedReal be parseFloat(cell).
            Return a new Ok with value parsedReal.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The Parse message 'P' (protocol.h, fe-exec.c PQsendPrepare): statement-name
cstring, query cstring, Int16 0 declared parameter types — the server infers
them, exactly libpq's paramTypes-NULL form.

## To pgParseMessage (stmtName: Text) and (sql: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable payload be a new Seq of Int.
    Let nb be pgCString(stmtName).
    Repeat for b in nb:
        Push b to payload.
    Let sqb be pgCString(sql).
    Repeat for b in sqb:
        Push b to payload.
    Let zeroTypes be pgI16be(0).
    Repeat for b in zeroTypes:
        Push b to payload.
    Return pgTaggedMessage(80, payload).

## Note
The Describe message 'D' for a prepared statement (variant 'S').

The Describe message 'D' for a prepared STATEMENT: variant byte 'S' then the
statement name — the server answers ParameterDescription then RowDescription
or NoData (postgres.c exec_describe_statement_message).

## To pgDescribeStatementMessage (stmtName: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable payload be a new Seq of Int.
    Push 83 to payload.
    Let nb be pgCString(stmtName).
    Repeat for b in nb:
        Push b to payload.
    Return pgTaggedMessage(68, payload).

## Note
The Describe message 'D' for a PORTAL: variant byte 'P' then the portal name
— the server answers RowDescription or NoData for the bound result.

## To pgDescribePortalMessage (portalName: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable payload be a new Seq of Int.
    Push 80 to payload.
    Let nb be pgCString(portalName).
    Repeat for b in nb:
        Push b to payload.
    Return pgTaggedMessage(68, payload).

## Note
The Bind message 'B' in text format: portal, statement, the text params (NULLs from the mask), one text result format.

The Bind message 'B' in text format (fe-exec.c PQsendQueryGuts): portal +
statement cstrings, Int16 0 parameter-format codes (all text, libpq's
paramFormats-NULL form), Int16 param count, then each param as Int32 byte
length + bytes — or Int32 -1 for NULL, driven by the nulls mask — and
Int16 1 + Int16 0 (one result-format code: text). The builder is total: a
mask entry beyond the mask's length reads as not-NULL (the Result-typed
callers validate lengths first).

## To pgBindMessage (portalName: Text) and (stmtName: Text) and (params: Seq of Text) and (nulls: Seq of Bool) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable payload be a new Seq of Int.
    Let pb be pgCString(portalName).
    Repeat for b in pb:
        Push b to payload.
    Let sb be pgCString(stmtName).
    Repeat for b in sb:
        Push b to payload.
    Let zeroFormats be pgI16be(0).
    Repeat for b in zeroFormats:
        Push b to payload.
    Let np be length of params.
    Let npBytes be pgI16be(np).
    Repeat for b in npBytes:
        Push b to payload.
    Let nn be length of nulls.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most np:
        Let mutable isNull be false.
        If i is at most nn:
            Set isNull to item i of nulls.
        If isNull is equal to true:
            Let negOne be pgI32be(-1).
            Repeat for b in negOne:
                Push b to payload.
        If isNull is equal to false:
            Let vb be text_bytes(item i of params).
            Let vn be length of vb.
            Let vlenBytes be pgI32be(vn).
            Repeat for b in vlenBytes:
                Push b to payload.
            Repeat for b in vb:
                Push b to payload.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Let oneResult be pgI16be(1).
    Repeat for b in oneResult:
        Push b to payload.
    Let textFormat be pgI16be(0).
    Repeat for b in textFormat:
        Push b to payload.
    Return pgTaggedMessage(66, payload).

## Note
The Execute message 'E': portal cstring + Int32 max_rows. This client always
sends 0 = fetch ALL rows (the backend treats max_rows <= 0 as FETCH_ALL,
postgres.c), so PortalSuspended never arrives.

## To pgExecuteMessage (portalName: Text) and (maxRows: Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable payload be a new Seq of Int.
    Let pb be pgCString(portalName).
    Repeat for b in pb:
        Push b to payload.
    Let mr be pgI32be(maxRows).
    Repeat for b in mr:
        Push b to payload.
    Return pgTaggedMessage(69, payload).

## Note
The Sync message 'S': an empty body — closes an extended-protocol flight and
makes the server answer ReadyForQuery even after an error.

## To pgSyncMessage () -> Seq of Int:
    Let payload be a new Seq of Int.
    Return pgTaggedMessage(83, payload).

## Note
The 16-byte CancelRequest packet (pqcomm.h CancelRequestPacket): Int32 16, Int32 80877102 (PG_PROTOCOL(1234,5678)), Int32 backend pid, Int32 secret key.

UNTAGGED — it is the first and only thing sent on a fresh connection.
## To pgCancelRequestMessage (pid: Int) and (secret: Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let lenB be pgI32be(16).
    Repeat for b in lenB:
        Push b to out.
    Let codeB be pgI32be(80877102).
    Repeat for b in codeB:
        Push b to out.
    Let pidB be pgI32be(pid).
    Repeat for b in pidB:
        Push b to out.
    Let secB be pgI32be(secret).
    Repeat for b in secB:
        Push b to out.
    Return out.

## Note
Parses a RowDescription 'T' body into (column names, type OIDs).

Parses a RowDescription 'T' body: Int16 field count, then per field the name
cstring, table oid Int32, attnum Int16, TYPE OID Int32, typlen Int16,
atttypmod Int32, format Int16 — returns (names, type oids).

## To pgParseRowDescription (body: Seq of Int) -> (Seq of Text, Seq of Int):
    Let mutable names be a new Seq of Text.
    Let mutable oids be a new Seq of Int.
    Let fieldCount be pgReadI16be(body, 1).
    Let mutable off be 3.
    If fieldCount is greater than 0:
        Repeat for f from 1 to fieldCount:
            Let (fname, afterName) be pgReadCStringAt(body, off).
            Push fname to names.
            Push pgReadI32be(body, afterName + 6) to oids.
            Set off to afterName + 18.
    Return (names, oids).

## Note
Parses a DataRow 'D' body: Int16 cell count, then per cell Int32 length + bytes, or length -1 for NULL.

Returns (cells, mask): a NULL cell is "" with mask true; a present EMPTY string
is "" with mask false — the distinction the nullMask exists to keep.
## To pgParseDataRow (body: Seq of Int) -> (Seq of Text, Seq of Bool):
    Let mutable cells be a new Seq of Text.
    Let mutable mask be a new Seq of Bool.
    Let colCount be pgReadI16be(body, 1).
    Let mutable roff be 3.
    If colCount is greater than 0:
        Repeat for cc from 1 to colCount:
            Let vlen be pgReadI32be(body, roff).
            Set roff to roff + 4.
            If vlen is less than 0:
                Push "" to cells.
                Push true to mask.
            If vlen is equal to 0:
                Push "" to cells.
                Push false to mask.
            If vlen is greater than 0:
                Let mutable vbytes be a new Seq of Int.
                Let vEnd be roff + vlen - 1.
                Let mutable vi be roff.
                While vi is at most vEnd:
                    Push item vi of body to vbytes.
                    Set vi to vi + 1.
                Push text_from_bytes(vbytes) to cells.
                Push false to mask.
                Set roff to roff + vlen.
    Return (cells, mask).

## Note
Parses a ParameterDescription 't' body: Int16 count + Int32 type OIDs.

## To pgParseParamDescription (body: Seq of Int) -> Seq of Int:
    Let mutable oids be a new Seq of Int.
    Let n be pgReadI16be(body, 1).
    Let mutable off be 3.
    If n is greater than 0:
        Repeat for i from 1 to n:
            Push pgReadI32be(body, off) to oids.
            Set off to off + 4.
    Return oids.

## Note
The `bool` type OID (16), verified against the catalog (pg_type.dat) like every
`pgOid*` constant below.

## To pgOidBool () -> Int:
    Return 16.

## Note
The `int8` (BIGINT) type OID (20).

## To pgOidInt8 () -> Int:
    Return 20.

## Note
The `int2` (SMALLINT) type OID (21).

## To pgOidInt2 () -> Int:
    Return 21.

## Note
The `int4` (INTEGER) type OID (23).

## To pgOidInt4 () -> Int:
    Return 23.

## Note
The `text` type OID (25).

## To pgOidText () -> Int:
    Return 25.

## Note
The `float8` (DOUBLE PRECISION) type OID (701).

## To pgOidFloat8 () -> Int:
    Return 701.

## Note
The `varchar` type OID (1043).

## To pgOidVarchar () -> Int:
    Return 1043.

## Note
The `numeric` type OID (1700).

## To pgOidNumeric () -> Int:
    Return 1700.

## Note
The `uuid` type OID (2950).

## To pgOidUuid () -> Int:
    Return 2950.

## Note
Collects the backend's reply to an extended-protocol flight into a result, draining to ReadyForQuery so the connection stays usable.

Collects the backend's reply to an extended-protocol flight: ParseComplete
'1', BindComplete '2', and CloseComplete '3' are acknowledgements;
RowDescription 'T' and DataRow 'D' build the result with its honest NULL
mask; CommandComplete 'C' carries the tag; NoData 'n' and EmptyQueryResponse
'I' are clean no-row outcomes; PortalSuspended 's' cannot arrive for our
Execute max_rows 0 but drains gracefully; an ErrorResponse becomes the Err
after the drain reaches ReadyForQuery, so the connection stays usable.

## To pgCollectExtended (c: Int) and (label: Text) -> Result of QueryResult and Text:
    Let mutable cols be a new Seq of Text.
    Let mutable oids be a new Seq of Int.
    Let mutable rws be a new Seq of Seq of Text.
    Let mutable masks be a new Seq of Seq of Bool.
    Let mutable tagVal be "".
    Let mutable errText be "".
    Let mutable looping be true.
    While looping is equal to true:
        Let mR be pgRecvMessage(c).
        Inspect mR:
            When Ok (frame):
                Let tg be item 1 of frame.
                Let body be pgFrameBody(frame).
                Let mutable handled be false.
                If tg is equal to 49:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 50:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 51:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 84:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Let (rdNames, rdOids) be pgParseRowDescription(body).
                    Set cols to rdNames.
                    Set oids to rdOids.
                If tg is equal to 110:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 68:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Let (rowCells, rowMask) be pgParseDataRow(body).
                    Push rowCells to rws.
                    Push rowMask to masks.
                If tg is equal to 67:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Let (tagText, tEnd) be pgReadCStringAt(body, 1).
                    Set tagVal to tagText.
                If tg is equal to 115:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 73:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 78:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 83:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 69:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set errText to pgErrorText(body).
                If tg is equal to 90:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set looping to false.
                If handled is equal to false:
                    Return a new Err with error "{label}: unexpected message tag {tg}".
            When Err (e):
                Return a new Err with error "{label}: {e}".
    If errText is equal to "":
        Let qr be a new QueryResult with columns cols and typeOids oids and rows rws and nullMask masks and cmdTag tagVal.
        Return a new Ok with value qr.
    Return a new Err with error errText.

## Note
Prepares a named statement: Parse + Describe('S')

+ Sync in one flight, then captures ParameterDescription's declared OIDs and the
result shape (RowDescription or NoData). An ErrorResponse (e.g. a syntax error)
drains to ReadyForQuery and surfaces as the Err — the connection stays usable.
## To pgPrepare (pgc: PgConn) and (stmtName: Text) and (sql: Text) -> Result of PgPrepared and Text:
    Let c be pgc's conn.
    Let flight be pgParseMessage(stmtName, sql) followed by pgDescribeStatementMessage(stmtName) followed by pgSyncMessage().
    Let sentF be tcpSend(c, flight).
    Inspect sentF:
        When Ok (n0):
            Let okf be 0.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "pgPrepare: {e}".
    Let mutable pOids be a new Seq of Int.
    Let mutable cols be a new Seq of Text.
    Let mutable oids be a new Seq of Int.
    Let mutable rowsFlag be false.
    Let mutable errText be "".
    Let mutable looping be true.
    While looping is equal to true:
        Let mR be pgRecvMessage(c).
        Inspect mR:
            When Ok (frame):
                Let tg be item 1 of frame.
                Let body be pgFrameBody(frame).
                Let mutable handled be false.
                If tg is equal to 49:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 116:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set pOids to pgParseParamDescription(body).
                If tg is equal to 84:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Let (rdNames, rdOids) be pgParseRowDescription(body).
                    Set cols to rdNames.
                    Set oids to rdOids.
                    Set rowsFlag to true.
                If tg is equal to 110:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set rowsFlag to false.
                If tg is equal to 78:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 83:
                    Set handled to true.
                If tg is equal to 69:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set errText to pgErrorText(body).
                If tg is equal to 90:
                    Set handled to true.
                    Set looping to false.
                If handled is equal to false:
                    Return a new Err with error "pgPrepare: unexpected message tag {tg}".
            When Err (e):
                Return a new Err with error "pgPrepare: {e}".
    If errText is equal to "":
        Let nameVal be stmtName.
        Let made be a new PgPrepared with stmtName nameVal and paramOids pOids and columns cols and typeOids oids and hasRows rowsFlag.
        Return a new Ok with value made.
    Return a new Err with error errText.

## Note
Executes a prepared statement with text-format params and an explicit nulls mask (true = send NULL for that param): Bind + Describe('P')

+ Execute (max_rows 0 = ALL) + Sync — libpq's PQsendQueryPrepared flight. The
mask must have exactly one entry per param.
## To pgExecPreparedN (pgc: PgConn) and (stmtName: Text) and (params: Seq of Text) and (nulls: Seq of Bool) -> Result of QueryResult and Text:
    Let np be length of params.
    Let nn be length of nulls.
    Let mutable lenOk be false.
    If nn is equal to np:
        Set lenOk to true.
    If lenOk is equal to false:
        Return a new Err with error "pg: the nulls mask must have one entry per param".
    Let c be pgc's conn.
    Let flight be pgBindMessage("", stmtName, params, nulls) followed by pgDescribePortalMessage("") followed by pgExecuteMessage("", 0) followed by pgSyncMessage().
    Let sentF be tcpSend(c, flight).
    Inspect sentF:
        When Ok (n0):
            Let okf be 0.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "pgExecPrepared: {e}".
    Return pgCollectExtended(c, "pgExecPrepared").

## Note
Executes a prepared statement with no NULL params — the common form,
delegating to pgExecPreparedN with an all-false mask.

## To pgExecPrepared (pgc: PgConn) and (stmtName: Text) and (params: Seq of Text) -> Result of QueryResult and Text:
    Let mutable noNulls be a new Seq of Bool.
    Repeat for p in params:
        Push false to noNulls.
    Return pgExecPreparedN(pgc, stmtName, params, noNulls).

## Note
The unnamed-statement fast path with an explicit nulls mask: Parse("") +
Bind + Describe('P') + Execute + Sync in ONE flight — libpq's
PQsendQueryParams (PQsendQueryGuts with the unnamed statement).

## To pgQueryParamsN (pgc: PgConn) and (sql: Text) and (params: Seq of Text) and (nulls: Seq of Bool) -> Result of QueryResult and Text:
    Let np be length of params.
    Let nn be length of nulls.
    Let mutable lenOk be false.
    If nn is equal to np:
        Set lenOk to true.
    If lenOk is equal to false:
        Return a new Err with error "pg: the nulls mask must have one entry per param".
    Let c be pgc's conn.
    Let flight be pgParseMessage("", sql) followed by pgBindMessage("", "", params, nulls) followed by pgDescribePortalMessage("") followed by pgExecuteMessage("", 0) followed by pgSyncMessage().
    Let sentF be tcpSend(c, flight).
    Inspect sentF:
        When Ok (n0):
            Let okf be 0.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "pgQueryParams: {e}".
    Return pgCollectExtended(c, "pgQueryParams").

## Note
The unnamed-statement fast path with no NULL params.

## To pgQueryParams (pgc: PgConn) and (sql: Text) and (params: Seq of Text) -> Result of QueryResult and Text:
    Let mutable noNulls be a new Seq of Bool.
    Repeat for p in params:
        Push false to noNulls.
    Return pgQueryParamsN(pgc, sql, params, noNulls).

## Note
True when the cell at (row, column) was SQL NULL, bounds-checked with the
same range errors as the cell getters.

## To pgIsNullAt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Bool and Text:
    Let masks be qr's nullMask.
    Let nr be length of masks.
    If rowIdx is less than 1:
        Return a new Err with error "pg: row {rowIdx} is out of range (1..{nr})".
    If rowIdx is greater than nr:
        Return a new Err with error "pg: row {rowIdx} is out of range (1..{nr})".
    Let mrow be item rowIdx of masks.
    Let nc be length of mrow.
    If colIdx is less than 1:
        Return a new Err with error "pg: column {colIdx} is out of range (1..{nc})".
    If colIdx is greater than nc:
        Return a new Err with error "pg: column {colIdx} is out of range (1..{nc})".
    Let flag be item colIdx of mrow.
    Return a new Ok with value flag.

## Note
The cell as an optional Text: `none` for SQL NULL, `some value` otherwise —
NULL is expressible, not spoofed as "".

## To pgTextAtOpt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Option of Text and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Let emptyOpt be none.
                Return a new Ok with value emptyOpt.
            Let cellR be pgCellAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
            Inspect cellR:
                When Ok (cell):
                    Let wrapped be some cell.
                    Return a new Ok with value wrapped.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The cell as an optional Int: `none` for NULL, the pgIntAt parse otherwise.

## To pgIntAtOpt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Option of Int and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Let emptyOpt be none.
                Return a new Ok with value emptyOpt.
            Let intR be pgIntAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
            Inspect intR:
                When Ok (v):
                    Let wrapped be some v.
                    Return a new Ok with value wrapped.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The cell as an optional Bool: `none` for NULL, the pgBoolAt parse otherwise.

## To pgBoolAtOpt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Option of Bool and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Let emptyOpt be none.
                Return a new Ok with value emptyOpt.
            Let boolR be pgBoolAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
            Inspect boolR:
                When Ok (v):
                    Let wrapped be some v.
                    Return a new Ok with value wrapped.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The cell as an optional Real: `none` for NULL, the pgRealAt parse otherwise.

## To pgRealAtOpt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Option of Real and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Let emptyOpt be none.
                Return a new Ok with value emptyOpt.
            Let realR be pgRealAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
            Inspect realR:
                When Ok (v):
                    Let wrapped be some v.
                    Return a new Ok with value wrapped.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
True when `s` has numeric_out's plain-decimal shape: optional minus, digits,
optionally a point with digits on BOTH sides. NaN and Infinity are rejected
(surface those as Errs, not fake numbers).

## To pgIsNumericText (s: Text) -> Bool:
    Let sb be text_bytes(s).
    Let n be length of sb.
    If n is equal to 0:
        Return false.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    If (item 1 of sb) is equal to 45:
        If n is equal to 1:
            Return false.
        Set i to 2.
    Let mutable sawDigit be false.
    Let mutable sawDot be false.
    While i is at most n:
        Let b be item i of sb.
        Let mutable okb be false.
        If b is at least 48:
            If b is at most 57:
                Set okb to true.
                Set sawDigit to true.
        If b is equal to 46:
            If sawDot is equal to false:
                If sawDigit is equal to true:
                    If i is less than n:
                        Set okb to true.
                        Set sawDot to true.
                        Set sawDigit to false.
        If okb is equal to false:
            Return false.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return sawDigit.

## Note
The cell as the EXACT decimal (arbitrary precision — 30+ digit numerics
round-trip digit for digit). NULL and non-numeric text are value-level Errs.

## To pgDecimalAt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Decimal and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Return a new Err with error "pg: the cell at row {rowIdx} column {colIdx} is NULL (use pgDecimalAtOpt)".
            Let cellR be pgCellAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
            Inspect cellR:
                When Ok (cell):
                    If pgIsNumericText(cell) is equal to false:
                        Return a new Err with error "pg: the value '{cell}' is not a numeric".
                    Let d be decimal(cell).
                    Return a new Ok with value d.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The cell as an optional exact decimal: `none` for NULL.

## To pgDecimalAtOpt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Option of Decimal and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Let emptyOpt be none.
                Return a new Ok with value emptyOpt.
            Let decR be pgDecimalAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
            Inspect decR:
                When Ok (v):
                    Let wrapped be some v.
                    Return a new Ok with value wrapped.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
True when `s` is the canonical 8-4-4-4-12 UUID text (uuid_out's shape),
either case: 36 bytes, dashes at positions 9/14/19/24, hex elsewhere.

## To pgIsUuidText (s: Text) -> Bool:
    Let sb be text_bytes(s).
    Let sn be length of sb.
    If sn is equal to 36:
        Let mutable i be 1.
        While i is at most 36:
            Let b be item i of sb.
            Let mutable isDash be false.
            If i is equal to 9:
                Set isDash to true.
            If i is equal to 14:
                Set isDash to true.
            If i is equal to 19:
                Set isDash to true.
            If i is equal to 24:
                Set isDash to true.
            Let mutable good be false.
            If isDash is equal to true:
                If b is equal to 45:
                    Set good to true.
            If isDash is equal to false:
                If b is at least 48:
                    If b is at most 57:
                        Set good to true.
                If b is at least 97:
                    If b is at most 102:
                        Set good to true.
                If b is at least 65:
                    If b is at most 70:
                        Set good to true.
            If good is equal to false:
                Return false.
            Set i to i + 1.
        Return true.
    Return false.

## Note
The cell as a Uuid (uuid.lg), case-normalized. NULL and malformed text are
value-level Errs.

## To pgUuidAt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Uuid and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Return a new Err with error "pg: the cell at row {rowIdx} column {colIdx} is NULL (use pgUuidAtOpt)".
            Let cellR be pgCellAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
            Inspect cellR:
                When Ok (cell):
                    If pgIsUuidText(cell) is equal to false:
                        Return a new Err with error "pg: the value '{cell}' is not a uuid".
                    Let low be lower(cell).
                    Let u be uuidParse(low).
                    Return a new Ok with value u.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The cell as an optional Uuid: `none` for NULL.

## To pgUuidAtOpt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Option of Uuid and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Let emptyOpt be none.
                Return a new Ok with value emptyOpt.
            Let uuidR be pgUuidAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
            Inspect uuidR:
                When Ok (v):
                    Let wrapped be some v.
                    Return a new Ok with value wrapped.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The cell as a PG enum label, asserted to be one of the expected labels (enum
values arrive as text; the expected set is yours to declare). NULL and an
unexpected label are value-level Errs.

## To pgEnumAt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) and (allowed: Seq of Text) -> Result of Text and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Return a new Err with error "pg: the cell at row {rowIdx} column {colIdx} is NULL (use pgEnumAtOpt)".
            Let cellR be pgCellAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
            Inspect cellR:
                When Ok (cell):
                    Let mutable found be false.
                    Repeat for lab in allowed:
                        If lab is equal to cell:
                            Set found to true.
                    If found is equal to false:
                        Return a new Err with error "pg: the value '{cell}' is not one of the expected enum values".
                    Return a new Ok with value cell.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The cell as an optional enum label: `none` for NULL.

## To pgEnumAtOpt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) and (allowed: Seq of Text) -> Result of Option of Text and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Let emptyOpt be none.
                Return a new Ok with value emptyOpt.
            Let enumR be pgEnumAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx, allowed).
            Inspect enumR:
                When Ok (v):
                    Let wrapped be some v.
                    Return a new Ok with value wrapped.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
The typed checkpoint: validates a cell against its column's declared OID before handing it back; NULL is an `Err`.

The typed checkpoint: dispatches on the column's declared OID and VALIDATES
the cell against it — ints must be integers, bool normalizes to true/false,
float4/float8 must be reals, numeric must be plain decimal, uuid normalizes
to lowercase; text/varchar and unknown OIDs pass through opaque. NULL is an
Err (ask pgIsNullAt first, or use the Opt getters).

## To pgTypedAt (qr: QueryResult) and (rowIdx: Int) and (colIdx: Int) -> Result of Text and Text:
    Let nullR be pgIsNullAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
    Inspect nullR:
        When Ok (isNull):
            If isNull is equal to true:
                Return a new Err with error "pg: the cell at row {rowIdx} column {colIdx} is NULL (use pgIsNullAt)".
            Let cellR be pgCellAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
            Inspect cellR:
                When Ok (cell):
                    Let toids be qr's typeOids.
                    Let ntoids be length of toids.
                    Let mutable oid be 0.
                    If colIdx is at most ntoids:
                        Set oid to item colIdx of toids.
                    If oid is equal to 16:
                        Let bR be pgBoolAt(qr, rowIdx, colIdx).
                        Inspect bR:
                            When Ok (bv):
                                If bv is equal to true:
                                    Return a new Ok with value "true".
                                Return a new Ok with value "false".
                            When Err (e):
                                Return a new Err with error e.
                    Let mutable isIntOid be false.
                    If oid is equal to 20:
                        Set isIntOid to true.
                    If oid is equal to 21:
                        Set isIntOid to true.
                    If oid is equal to 23:
                        Set isIntOid to true.
                    If isIntOid is equal to true:
                        If pgIsIntText(cell) is equal to false:
                            Return a new Err with error "pg: the value '{cell}' is not an Int".
                        Return a new Ok with value cell.
                    Let mutable isRealOid be false.
                    If oid is equal to 700:
                        Set isRealOid to true.
                    If oid is equal to 701:
                        Set isRealOid to true.
                    If isRealOid is equal to true:
                        If pgIsRealText(cell) is equal to false:
                            Return a new Err with error "pg: the value '{cell}' is not a Real".
                        Return a new Ok with value cell.
                    If oid is equal to 1700:
                        If pgIsNumericText(cell) is equal to false:
                            Return a new Err with error "pg: the value '{cell}' is not a numeric".
                        Return a new Ok with value cell.
                    If oid is equal to 2950:
                        If pgIsUuidText(cell) is equal to false:
                            Return a new Err with error "pg: the value '{cell}' is not a uuid".
                        Let low be lower(cell).
                        Return a new Ok with value low.
                    Return a new Ok with value cell.
                When Err (e):
                    Return a new Err with error e.
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error e.

## Note
Opens a transaction at the session's default isolation. Thin over pgExecute
("BEGIN" is from the validated closed set, never user data).

## To pgBegin (pgc: PgConn) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Return pgExecute(pgc, "BEGIN").

## Note
Opens a transaction at an EXPLICIT isolation level.

The level is validated against the closed set {"read committed", "repeatable
read", "serializable"} BEFORE any SQL is composed — an unknown level never
reaches the server.
## To pgBeginIsolation (pgc: PgConn) and (level: Text) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Let mutable okLevel be false.
    If level is equal to "read committed":
        Set okLevel to true.
    If level is equal to "repeatable read":
        Set okLevel to true.
    If level is equal to "serializable":
        Set okLevel to true.
    If okLevel is equal to false:
        Return a new Err with error "pgBeginIsolation: the isolation level must be 'read committed', 'repeatable read', or 'serializable'".
    Return pgExecute(pgc, "BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL {level}").

## Note
Commits the open transaction. A serialization failure (SQLSTATE 40001) at
commit arrives as an ordinary Err — retry the transaction.

## To pgCommit (pgc: PgConn) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Return pgExecute(pgc, "COMMIT").

## Note
Rolls the open transaction back, discarding its writes.

## To pgRollback (pgc: PgConn) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Return pgExecute(pgc, "ROLLBACK").

## Note
Sets this session's statement_timeout in milliseconds (0 disables).

A statement that exceeds it is cancelled by the server with SQLSTATE 57014
(query_canceled), which surfaces as an ordinary Err. Only the validated integer
is interpolated — never text.
## To pgSetStatementTimeout (pgc: PgConn) and (millis: Int) -> Result of Int and Text:
    If millis is less than 0:
        Return a new Err with error "pgSetStatementTimeout: the timeout must be at least 0 milliseconds".
    Return pgExecute(pgc, "SET statement_timeout = {millis}").

## Note
Fires the cancel protocol at the connection's backend WHEN A QUERY IS GENUINELY IN FLIGHT — the `inFlight` guard is the correlation the cancel needs.

A cancel is routed to the backend by its PID (docs, protocol-flow "Canceling
Requests in Progress"), and that PID serves the session's NEXT statement too; so
a cancel dispatched after the target already finished races the following query
and can cancel IT. When `inFlight` is false the target is not running: this
opens NO socket and is a clean `Ok` no-op — a stale cancel can never disturb the
next query. When `inFlight` is true it opens a FRESH connection carrying only
the 16-byte CancelRequest packet and waits for the postmaster to close it — the
server never replies, and its close says the backend has been signalled — before
closing its own end, as libpq's PQcancel does: a statement sent after this
returns reaches a backend whose signal already arrived while it waited for a
command, which ignores it, so the cancel never lands on the statement after it.
Best-effort by design: even a correctly-correlated dispatch is no guarantee the
statement dies. Requires the BackendKeyData the startup captured.
## To pgCancelInFlight (pgc: PgConn) and (inFlight: Bool) -> Result of Unit and Text:
    If inFlight is equal to false:
        Return a new Ok with value nothing.
    Let hostVal be pgc's host.
    Let portVal be pgc's port.
    Let cR be tcpConnect(hostVal, portVal).
    Inspect cR:
        When Ok (c2):
            Let pidVal be pgc's backendPid.
            Let secretVal be pgc's backendSecret.
            Let pkt be pgCancelRequestMessage(pidVal, secretVal).
            Let sentP be tcpSend(c2, pkt).
            Inspect sentP:
                When Ok (n0):
                    Let processed be tcpRecv(c2, 1).
                    Return tcpClose(c2).
                When Err (e):
                    Let closedAnyway be tcpClose(c2).
                    Return a new Err with error "pgCancelRequest: {e}".
        When Err (e):
            Return a new Err with error "pgCancelRequest: {e}".

## Note
The best-effort cancel for a query the caller KNOWS is in flight (issued from a separate task while `pgQuery`/`pgExecute` blocks on this connection).

Thin over `pgCancelInFlight(pgc, true)` — the byte-exact CancelRequest fires.
Correlate with `pgCancelInFlight` and an in-flight token whenever a stale cancel
could otherwise race the connection's next statement.
## To pgCancelRequest (pgc: PgConn) -> Result of Unit and Text:
    Return pgCancelInFlight(pgc, true).
